WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.979
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:09.164
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:16.389
Unit fifteen: Stakeholder Engagement. Today you will describe slow processes with over time / gradually adverbials.

4
00:00:16.689 --> 00:00:19.260
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.317
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.183 --> 00:00:25.161
Stakeholder.

7
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:30.019
Stakeholder means anyone affected by or able to affect a decision.

8
00:00:30.617 --> 00:00:33.928
Fishing communities are key stakeholders in the dam project.

9
00:00:34.517 --> 00:00:37.192
Your turn. Say: Stakeholder.

10
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:41.278
Stakeholder.

11
00:00:42.017 --> 00:00:43.621
Engagement strategy.

12
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:48.880
Engagement strategy means a plan for involving stakeholders meaningfully.

13
00:00:49.483 --> 00:00:52.725
The engagement strategy included village meetings in Khmer.

14
00:00:53.317 --> 00:00:56.120
Your turn. Say: Engagement strategy.

15
00:00:58.967 --> 00:01:00.862
Engagement strategy.

16
00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:02.804
Consultation.

17
00:01:03.817 --> 00:01:07.581
Consultation means seeking people's views before making a decision.

18
00:01:08.183 --> 00:01:11.611
Genuine consultation happens before decisions are final.

19
00:01:12.217 --> 00:01:14.648
Your turn. Say: Consultation.

20
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:18.675
Consultation.

21
00:01:19.417 --> 00:01:20.429
Feedback loop.

22
00:01:21.417 --> 00:01:25.610
Feedback loop means a cycle in which responses inform further action.

23
00:01:26.217 --> 00:01:29.807
A feedback loop showed residents how their input changed the plan.

24
00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:33.092
Your turn. Say: Feedback loop.

25
00:01:35.933 --> 00:01:37.109
Feedback loop.

26
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:39.118
Transparency.

27
00:01:40.117 --> 00:01:43.556
Transparency means openness about decisions and their reasons.

28
00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:47.218
Transparency about compensation reduced suspicion.

29
00:01:47.817 --> 00:01:50.690
Your turn. Say: Transparency.

30
00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:54.732
Transparency.

31
00:01:55.483 --> 00:01:57.077
Trust-building.

32
00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:01.859
Trust-building means actions that develop mutual confidence over time.

33
00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.506
Trust-building began with small promises kept.

34
00:02:06.117 --> 00:02:08.653
Your turn. Say: Trust-building.

35
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.233
Trust-building.

36
00:02:13.983 --> 00:02:14.868
Alignment.

37
00:02:15.883 --> 00:02:18.997
Alignment means agreement on shared goals and direction.

38
00:02:19.583 --> 00:02:23.023
Alignment between the company and villagers took two years.

39
00:02:23.617 --> 00:02:26.199
Your turn. Say: Alignment.

40
00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:29.976
Alignment.

41
00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:31.904
Collaboration.

42
00:02:32.917 --> 00:02:35.961
Collaboration means working jointly toward a common aim.

43
00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:40.279
Collaboration with local NGOs improved the project's design.

44
00:02:40.883 --> 00:02:44.221
Your turn. Say: Collaboration.

45
00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:48.416
Collaboration.

46
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.990
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:53.850 --> 00:03:00.438
Visal, a mining company held one big meeting with villagers and now claims they 'consulted the community'. Is that enough?

48
00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:08.765
Hardly. Trust builds gradually through consistent engagement, not through a single event that ticks a box.

49
00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:11.873
What would genuine engagement look like?

50
00:03:12.983 --> 00:03:23.797
Regular meetings over time, in Khmer, with a feedback loop showing how concerns changed the plan. Otherwise, people stop believing their voices matter.

51
00:03:24.883 --> 00:03:26.848
And if the villagers remain opposed?

52
00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:38.102
Then the company must listen seriously. Over time, alignment may emerge, or the company may need to accept that the project is unacceptable.

53
00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:41.220
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:42.083 --> 00:03:45.279
Grammar focus: Over time and gradually adverbials.

55
00:03:45.579 --> 00:03:46.696
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:47.383 --> 00:03:50.068
Trust builds gradually through consistent engagement.

57
00:03:50.983 --> 00:03:54.155
Over time, alignment may emerge through regular dialogue.

58
00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:58.234
Credibility grows step by step via promises kept.

59
00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:10.177
Adverbials of gradual change can open a sentence for emphasis (Over time, …) or follow the verb (builds gradually). Use the adverb 'gradually', not the adjective 'gradual', to modify a verb.

60
00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:14.324
Be careful! Don't say: Trust builds gradual.

61
00:04:16.267 --> 00:04:18.220
Say: Trust builds gradually.

62
00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:20.229
Use the adverb 'gradually'.

63
00:04:21.033 --> 00:04:23.883
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:24.667 --> 00:04:26.655
Consultation or Box-Ticking?.

65
00:04:26.955 --> 00:04:32.878
Stakeholder engagement has become a standard requirement for major infrastructure and development projects.

66
00:04:33.867 --> 00:04:39.686
Yet critics argue that many consultations are performative, held after key decisions have already been made.

67
00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:48.702
Genuine engagement involves listening early, explaining trade-offs transparently and demonstrating, over time, how input has shaped outcomes.

68
00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:56.785
A well-designed feedback loop is crucial, because people participate only when they believe participation makes a difference.

69
00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:05.593
Trust, once damaged, is rebuilt only gradually, so organisations that engage authentically from the outset save both time and reputation.

70
00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:10.551
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:11.417 --> 00:05:13.823
The river's level rose blank during the night.

72
00:05:14.123 --> 00:05:17.718
A, gradual. B, gradually. C, graduation.

73
00:05:21.267 --> 00:05:23.696
The answer is B: gradually.

74
00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:28.266
A cycle in which responses inform further action is a blank.

75
00:05:28.566 --> 00:05:32.916
A, feedback loop. B, consultation. C, stakeholder.

76
00:05:36.467 --> 00:05:39.070
The answer is A: feedback loop.

77
00:05:40.217 --> 00:05:43.122
Agreement on shared goals and direction is blank.

78
00:05:43.422 --> 00:05:48.004
A, transparency. B, collaboration. C, alignment.

79
00:05:51.567 --> 00:05:53.531
The answer is C: alignment.

80
00:05:54.683 --> 00:05:56.311
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:56.611 --> 00:06:05.222
A, Over time, relationships improved.. B, Over the time, relationships improved gradual.. C, Over time relationships improving..

82
00:06:08.767 --> 00:06:12.287
The answer is A: Over time, relationships improved..

83
00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:17.319
According to the reading, critics say many consultations are…

84
00:06:17.619 --> 00:06:21.458
A, performative. B, too long. C, too early.

85
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.359
The answer is A: performative.

86
00:06:28.567 --> 00:06:31.834
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:32.683 --> 00:06:35.983
Why is a feedback loop important when engaging stakeholders?

88
00:06:52.783 --> 00:06:54.284
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:54.584 --> 00:07:31.041
A feedback loop is important because it proves to stakeholders that their participation genuinely influences decisions. Without it, consultation feels like a formality, and people gradually withdraw their trust and cooperation. When an organisation reports back, explaining which suggestions were adopted and why others were not, it demonstrates respect and accountability. For example, a city could show residents how their comments changed a bus route. Over time, such transparency turns sceptics into partners.

90
00:07:31.341 --> 00:07:33.085
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:33.983 --> 00:07:38.865
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and over time and gradually adverbials.

92
00:07:39.165 --> 00:07:44.673
Today's value is trust-building. Earn trust slowly, through honest words and kept promises.

93
00:07:44.973 --> 00:07:47.800
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.740
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

